/*--------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
/*--------------------------- File priority.dox ----------------------------*/
/*--------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
/** \addtogroup area_priority */
/** \defgroup theory_priority Theory of priority queues
 \ingroup area_priority

 \details The headers of `include/ADS/priority` realize the PRIORITYQUEUE of
 section 10.1 of the book, on the lists, the trees and the heaps that the book
 compares, and on several structures beyond it: the Fibonacci heap that
 section 11.5 names for the shortest paths, the pairing heap, the heaps of
 plain items without handles, the monotone queues of integer keys, the
 double-ended queues, and the structures that merge \f$ k \f$ sorted
 sequences, the tree of losers among them, on which the sequence heap of
 Sanders rests. This page collects what the headers take for granted: the
 definitions and the notation shared by all of them (see
 \ref theory_priority_notation), the realizations and the costs of their
 operations (see \ref theory_priority_repr), and, for each family of
 structures, the invariant on which its correctness rests and the argument
 behind its bounds (from \ref theory_priority_heaps to
 \ref theory_priority_merge). The pseudocode of each operation is instead on
 the page of its header, in the comment of its function, together with the
 section of the book, or the paper, that it follows.

 \tableofcontents

 \section theory_priority_notation Definitions and notation

 A priority queue holds a multiset of \f$ n \f$ items, each \f$ x \f$ with a
 priority \f$ p( x ) \f$ taken from a totally ordered set, and offers
 `insert( x , p )`, which adds an item, `min()`, which reads an item of
 least priority, and `deleteMin()`, which removes it; the PRIORITYQUEUE of
 the book (section 10.1, the concept `PriorityQueue` of `PriorityQueue.h`)
 has also `decrease( y , p )`, which lowers to \f$ p \f$ the priority of the
 item that the <em>handle</em> \f$ y \f$ names, the handle being what
 `insert()` gave for it (a cell, a node, an index). The items of equal
 priority leave in no given order unless a header says otherwise. A queue
 without handles (`DaryHeap`, `MinMaxHeap`, `SequenceHeap`, the monotone
 queues) has no `decrease()`: an item whose priority improves is pushed
 again, and its stale copy is skipped when it comes out (the
 <em>lazy deletion</em>), which costs \f$ O( m ) \f$ items in the queue
 instead of \f$ O( n ) \f$ for \f$ m \f$ decreases. Several queues order
 their items by a predicate `better( a , b )`, true if \f$ a \f$ must come
 out before \f$ b \f$, with `std::less` giving the least item first.

 A queue is <em>monotone</em> if the priorities pushed are never smaller
 than the last one extracted, \f$ \ell \f$; Dijkstra's algorithm with
 nonnegative costs is monotone, since the distance of a node extracted is
 not smaller than that of the node extracted before it, and so is the
 simulation of discrete events. The monotone queues of integer keys use
 this property to find the minimum without comparisons.

 <table>
 <caption>The symbols of the headers of the area</caption>
 <tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ n \f$, \f$ p( x ) \f$</td>
     <td>the number of items in the queue, and the priority (or key) of the
         item \f$ x \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ d \f$, \f$ D \f$</td>
     <td>the arity of a heap, the number of children of each node, 2 for the
         binary heap of the book</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ h \f$</td>
     <td>the height of a heap, the number of its levels less one</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ \ell \f$</td>
     <td>the last key extracted from a monotone queue, below which no key is
         pushed</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ W \f$, \f$ B \f$, \f$ C \f$</td>
     <td>the bits of a key of a radix heap (the buckets of the window of the
         queue of Dial), the bits of one of its digits, and the width of the
         band \f$ [ \ell , \ell + C ] \f$ of the keys pushed</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ k \f$</td>
     <td>the number of sequences merged, or of players of a tree of
         losers</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ M \f$, \f$ B \f$</td>
     <td>in the model of the cache, the items that the cache holds and those
         of a block, i.e., of a line or of a page</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ F_k \f$, \f$ \phi \f$</td>
     <td>the \f$ k \f$-th number of Fibonacci, \f$ F_0 = 0 \f$,
         \f$ F_1 = 1 \f$, and the golden ratio
         \f$ \phi = ( 1 + \sqrt{5} ) / 2 \f$</td></tr>
 </table>

 A cost is <em>amortized</em> when it holds for the average of any sequence of
 operations from the empty queue, though a single operation may cost more;
 the bounds of the Fibonacci, pairing and radix heaps, and those of the
 queues that sort a run of removals, are of this kind, and they are proved
 by a potential \f$ \Phi \geq 0 \f$, zero on the empty queue, whose increase
 pays for the cheap operations and whose decrease pays for the dear ones.

 \section theory_priority_repr Realizations and their costs

 The library has three realizations of the PRIORITYQUEUE of the book, which
 section 10.1 compares, and several beyond it, which differ in the bounds,
 in the operations, and in the way they use the memory.

