 The list sorted pays at the insertion the scan that finds the place of the
 item, and the unsorted one pays it at the extraction, which scans for the
 minimum; the list is usually preferable only for a few items, or when one of
 the two operations is much rarer than the other. The Red-Black tree keeps
 the pairs \f$ ( p( x ) , i ) \f$, \f$ i \f$ the number of insertion, which
 makes the keys distinct, and its height \f$ O( \log n ) \f$ bounds every
 operation; it is the realization of the book that also visits the items in
 order. The heap of the book has the same bounds with better constants, and
 is usually preferable: its items lie in a vector, the minimum is at its
 root, and only one path from the root is walked by each operation (see
 \ref theory_priority_heaps). The Fibonacci and the pairing heaps lower the
 cost of `decrease()`, which matters when the decreases outnumber the
 extractions, as in Dijkstra's and Prim's algorithms on dense graphs (see
 \ref theory_priority_amortized). The monotone queues and the merges are
 described in \ref theory_priority_monotone and \ref theory_priority_merge.

 \section theory_priority_heaps Heaps

 A <em>heap</em> is a tree in which each node has a priority not smaller than
 that of its parent; hence its root holds a minimum, and `min()` costs
 \f$ O( 1 ) \f$. The heap of section 10.1.3 (`HeapPriorityQueue.h`) is a
 \f$ d \f$-ary tree full but for the rightmost leaves of its last level,
 stored by levels in a vector \f$ H[ 0 .. n - 1 ] \f$, so that the children
 of the place \f$ i \f$ are \f$ d i + 1 , \ldots , d i + d \f$ and its parent
 is \f$ \lfloor ( i - 1 ) / d \rfloor \f$, with no pointer stored.

 <em>Lemma 1 (the height of a heap).</em> A \f$ d \f$-ary heap of
 \f$ n \ge 1 \f$ items has
 \f[
   h = \lceil \log_d ( ( d - 1 ) n + 1 ) \rceil - 1 \tag{1}
 \f]
 levels below its root.

 Indeed, its first \f$ h \f$ levels are full and hold
 \f$ 1 + d + \cdots + d^{h-1} = ( d^h - 1 ) / ( d - 1 ) \f$ items, fewer than
 \f$ n \f$, while the full levels up to the last would hold
 \f$ ( d^{h+1} - 1 ) / ( d - 1 ) \ge n \f$; taking the logarithms gives (1),
 i.e., \f$ h = \Theta( \log_d n ) \f$.

 `insert()` puts the new item in the first free place, a leaf, and moves it
 up while it is smaller than its parent; the heap is violated only between
 the item and its parent, and each swap moves the violation one level up,
 so that at most \f$ h \f$ swaps, of one comparison each, restore the order.
 `decrease()` does the same from the place of the item, which the item keeps
 up to date at each swap, so that the handle finds it in \f$ O( 1 ) \f$.
 `deleteMin()` moves the last leaf into the root and down
 (`minHeapRestore()` of the book), swapping it with its smallest child while
 that child is smaller; the violation now lies between the item and its
 children, and each level costs \f$ d \f$ comparisons, i.e.,
 \f$ O( d \log_d n ) \f$ in all.

 With \f$ m \f$ decreases and \f$ n \f$ extractions, as in Dijkstra's
 algorithm, a \f$ d \f$-ary heap costs
 \f[
   O( m \log_d n + n d \log_d n ) , \tag{2}
 \f]
 which is least for \f$ d \f$ near \f$ \max\{ 2 , m / n \} \f$, i.e.,
 \f$ O( m \log_{2 + m / n} n ) \f$ (Johnson); on the dense graphs this is
 \f$ O( m ) \f$, as the Fibonacci heap gives. In practice \f$ d = 4 \f$ is
 usually preferable even when (2) would ask for \f$ d = 2 \f$, since the four
 children of a node lie in one line of the cache and the heap is half as
 deep (LaMarca and Ladner; Larkin, Sen and Tarjan): this is the default of
 `DaryHeap` and of `IndexedDaryHeap`, which keep the keys in the vector
 itself, so that a comparison never follows a pointer, and replace the
 handles by plain items (`DaryHeap`) or by the vector of the places of the
 ids \f$ [ 0 , n ) \f$ (`IndexedDaryHeap`). The walk down of `deleteMin()`
 compares the item carried down with the best child at each level; the
 walk from the bottom up of Floyd and Wegener (the `BottomUp` of `DaryHeap`)
 moves instead the hole of the root down to a leaf by the best children,
 \f$ d - 1 \f$ comparisons per level, and then the item up from there. The
 item carried down is the last leaf, which usually belongs near the bottom,
 so that the climb from the leaf is short and the walk saves about one
 comparison per level; it loses when the item would stop halfway.

 A search that prunes or reorders its frontier needs more than
 `decrease()`: the removal of any item and the raise of its key.
 `IndexedDaryHeap` raises the key of an id by the walk down of `pop()` from
 its place, and removes an id by moving the last item into its place and
 then up or down.

 <em>Lemma 2 (the hole of a removal).</em> Let the item at the place
 \f$ i \f$ of a heap be removed and the last item \f$ x \f$ be put in its
 place. If \f$ x \f$ is smaller than the parent of \f$ i \f$, the walk up
 from \f$ i \f$ restores the heap, and otherwise the walk down does; one
 walk alone is needed, and the removal costs \f$ O( d \log_d n ) \f$.

 Indeed, the items below \f$ i \f$ are not smaller than the item removed,
 which is not smaller than the parent of \f$ i \f$; if \f$ x \f$ is
 smaller than that parent, it is smaller than all of them, and the heap is
 violated only between \f$ x \f$ and its ancestors, which the walk up
 mends as in `insert()`. Otherwise \f$ x \f$ is not smaller than its
 parent, the heap is violated only between \f$ x \f$ and its children
 (\f$ x \f$ may come from another subtree, where it was larger than the
 items below \f$ i \f$), and the walk down mends it as in `deleteMin()`.

 The searches that select a node by one key and must know the least of a
 second one over the whole frontier (a bounded suboptimal search, which
 expands by an inflated cost or by the distance to the goal and stops by
 the least lower bound \f$ f_{\mathrm{min}} \f$; a branch and bound by
 best estimate, which prunes by the least bound of the open nodes) keep
 the ids in two such heaps, by the key and by the bound
 (`IndexedHeapPair`): the two minima are read in \f$ O( 1 ) \f$, and a
 `pop()` by the key removes the id from the heap of the bounds by
 Lemma 2, so that every operation costs two walks,
 \f$ O( d \log_d n ) \f$. A heap of the bounds with lazy deletion, in
 which the ids popped stay until they reach its top, saves the second walk
 of `pop()` but keeps up to \f$ O( m ) \f$ stale entries for \f$ m \f$
 operations and needs a stamp per entry to tell the copies of an id
 pushed again; the pair keeps exactly \f$ n \f$ entries in each heap and
 changes the bound of an id where it is.

 An ordered frontier, read in order from its least item and changed
 anywhere (a parallel search that looks among the first items for one it
 may expand, or a list of free blocks kept by their least key), is not a
 heap but an ordered dictionary with iterators: of the trees of ADS,
 `BTreeMap` has the iteration from the least key and the removal by an
 iterator, `erase( it )`, which gives the item after it, besides
 `lowerBound()`, along \f$ O( \log n ) \f$ nodes of a few lines of cache (see
 \ref theory_tree_btree), while `SkipList`, `BTree` and `AVLTree`
 remove by the key alone. A frontier that is only popped is better kept
 in a heap, whose items need no order beyond the minimum.

 The heap with pointers of `LinkedHeapPriorityQueue.h` keeps the same shape
 on nodes linked to their parent and children. The node \f$ k \f$ of the
 numbering by levels from 1 is reached from the root by the bits of
 \f$ k \f$ after the leading one, 0 to the left and 1 to the right, since the
 children of the node \f$ k \f$ are \f$ 2 k \f$ and \f$ 2 k + 1 \f$; thus the
 place of the next leaf and that of the last one are found in
 \f$ O( \log n ) \f$, and the bounds are those of the vector, with
 \f$ d = 2 \f$.

 \section theory_priority_amortized Fibonacci and pairing heaps

 A Fibonacci heap (`FibonacciHeapPriorityQueue.h`) is a collection of heap
 ordered trees, whose roots lie in a circular list with a pointer to the
 least of them. `insert()` adds a tree of one node to the list, and
 `decrease()` cuts the node from its parent if it became smaller than it,
 making it a root; a node that loses a second child since it became a child
 is cut in turn (the <em>cascading cut</em>, a <em>mark</em> recording the
 first loss). `deleteMin()` is the only operation that tidies the heap: it
 moves the children of the minimum among the roots and then
 <em>consolidates</em> them, linking two roots of equal degree, the larger
 under the smaller, until the degrees of the roots differ. With \f$ t \f$ the
 number of trees and \f$ \mu \f$ the number of marked nodes, the potential
 \f[
   \Phi = t + 2 \mu \tag{3}
 \f]
 gives the bounds of Fredman and Tarjan. `insert()` raises \f$ \Phi \f$ by 1
 and costs \f$ O( 1 ) \f$. A `decrease()` that makes \f$ c \f$ cuts costs
 \f$ O( c ) \f$, adds \f$ c \f$ trees and removes the marks of at least
 \f$ c - 1 \f$ nodes, marking one more; hence \f$ \Phi \f$ changes by at most
 \f$ c + 2 ( 2 - c ) = 4 - c \f$, and the amortized cost is \f$ O( 1 ) \f$.
 The consolidation of \f$ t \f$ roots costs \f$ O( t + D ) \f$, with
 \f$ D \f$ the largest degree, and leaves at most \f$ D + 1 \f$ trees; hence
 `deleteMin()` costs \f$ O( D ) \f$ amortized, and the next lemma bounds
 \f$ D \f$.

 <em>Lemma 3 (the degrees of a Fibonacci heap).</em> A node of degree
 \f$ k \f$ has at least
 \f[
   F_{k+2} \ge \phi^k \tag{4}
 \f]
 descendants, itself included; hence \f$ D \le \log_\phi n \f$.

 In fact, take the children of a node \f$ x \f$ in the order in which they
 were linked to it. When the \f$ i \f$-th child was linked, \f$ x \f$ had
 at least \f$ i - 1 \f$ children, and the child had the same degree, since
 only the roots of equal degree are linked; it has lost at most one child
 since then, or it would have been cut, so that its degree is at least
 \f$ i - 2 \f$. By induction on the degree, the sizes \f$ s_k \f$ satisfy
 \f$ s_k \ge 2 + \sum_{i=2}^{k} s_{i-2} \f$, whose solution is
 \f$ F_{k+2} \f$, and \f$ F_{k+2} \ge \phi^k \f$ by the recurrence of the
 numbers of Fibonacci. Thus the heap gives
 \f$ O( m + n \log n ) \f$ for the shortest paths of Dijkstra (see
 \ref theory_graph_sp), against the \f$ O( m \log n ) \f$ of a binary heap;
 in practice its constants are larger, and a heap in a vector is usually
 faster but on large and dense graphs.

 A pairing heap (`PairingHeap.h`) is one heap ordered tree, whose nodes keep
 their leftmost child and their siblings. Two trees are <em>linked</em> by
 making the root of larger priority the leftmost child of the other one, in
 one comparison. `insert()` links a tree of one node with the root, and
 `decrease()` cuts the subtree of the node and links it with the root;
 `deleteMin()` removes the root and joins its children by two passes, the
 first linking them in pairs from the left and the second linking the
 results from the right. No balance is kept, and the analysis is that of a
 self-adjusting structure: `deleteMin()` costs \f$ O( \log n ) \f$
 amortized (Fredman, Sedgewick, Sleator and Tarjan), `insert()`
 \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ (Iacono) and `decrease()`
 \f$ O( 2^{2 \sqrt{\log \log n}} ) \f$ (Pettie), and Fredman proved that
 \f$ \Omega( \log \log n ) \f$ amortized is needed by `decrease()` in this
 family of heaps, against the \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ of the Fibonacci heap. In the
 experiments the pairing heap is nevertheless faster than the Fibonacci heap
 (Stasko and Vitter; Larkin, Sen and Tarjan). The auxiliary two-pass variant
 of Stasko and Vitter (the `Aux` of `PairingHeap`) keeps the trees of
 `insert()` and `decrease()` in a list beside the root, which `deleteMin()`
 joins by the multipass pairing, i.e., linking the trees in pairs from the
 front of a queue and putting the result at its back, until one tree is
 left; it pays when the insertions outnumber the extractions.

 The pairing heap removes any node by the delete of Fredman et al.: the
 node is cut from its siblings, its children are joined by the two passes
 of `deleteMin()`, and the tree that results is linked with the root (or
 put in the list beside it with `Aux`), so that `erase()` costs as a
 `deleteMin()` on the subtree of the node, \f$ O( \log n ) \f$
 amortized, the potential of the analysis counting the trees joined there
 as it counts those of the root. A raise of the priority cannot leave the
 node where it is, since its children may now be smaller than it: the
 node is taken out in the same way, its children joined in its place, and
 inserted again alone, with the same handle (`increase()`), at the same
 cost.

 \section theory_priority_monotone Monotone queues of integer keys

 On a monotone queue whose keys are integers, the minimum can be found by
 the position of the keys rather than by comparisons. The queue of Dial
 (`DialQueue.h`) keeps the items with key \f$ k \f$ in the bucket
 \f$ k \bmod W \f$ of a circular window of \f$ W \f$ buckets. If every key
 pushed lies in \f$ [ \ell , \ell + C ] \f$ with \f$ C < W \f$, the keys in
 the queue lie in \f$ [ \ell , \ell + W ) \f$, and two of them in one bucket
 are equal; hence the minimum is in the first bucket in use from that of
 \f$ \ell \f$ around the circle, found by a scan of a bit per bucket, i.e.,
 of \f$ W / 64 \f$ words. A key beyond the window goes to a radix heap, from
 which the items move into the window when the window reaches them.

 The radix heap of Ahuja, Mehlhorn, Orlin and Tarjan (`RadixHeap.h`) splits
 a key \f$ x \f$ of \f$ W \f$ bits into digits of \f$ B \f$ bits and puts it
 in the bucket of index
 \f[
   2^B r + x_r , \qquad r \text{ the digit of the highest bit in which } x
   \text{ and } \ell \text{ differ} , \tag{5}
 \f]
 \f$ x_r \f$ the digit \f$ r \f$ of \f$ x \f$, and \f$ r = 0 \f$ if
 \f$ x = \ell \f$.

 <em>Lemma 4 (the order of the buckets).</em> With \f$ x , y \ge \ell \f$,
 if the bucket of \f$ x \f$ has a smaller index than that of \f$ y \f$, then
 \f$ x < y \f$; and the keys of a bucket of the row \f$ r = 0 \f$ are all
 equal.

 Indeed, \f$ x \f$ agrees with \f$ \ell \f$ above its digit \f$ r \f$, and
 its digit \f$ r \f$ is larger than that of \f$ \ell \f$ when \f$ r > 0 \f$,
 since \f$ x > \ell \f$. If \f$ y \f$ lies in a higher row \f$ r' > r \f$,
 then \f$ x \f$ agrees with \f$ \ell \f$ on the digit \f$ r' \f$ and above,
 while \f$ y \f$ agrees with \f$ \ell \f$ above \f$ r' \f$ and exceeds it on
 the digit \f$ r' \f$; hence \f$ y > x \f$. If \f$ y \f$ lies in the same
 row, both agree with \f$ \ell \f$ above the digit \f$ r \f$, and the
 digits \f$ r \f$, i.e., the indices of the buckets, decide. In the row 0,
 \f$ x \f$ and \f$ \ell \f$ differ only in the last digit, which is the
 index of the bucket.

 Hence the minimum is in the first bucket in use, which a mask of the
 buckets finds by its lowest bit. If that bucket lies in a row
 \f$ r > 0 \f$, its least key becomes the new \f$ \ell \f$, and its items
 move to the buckets that (5) gives for it, all in rows below \f$ r \f$,
 while the other buckets keep their items. An item thus only moves to lower
 rows, at most \f$ \lceil W / B \rceil - 1 \f$ times, and with the potential
 \f$ \Phi = \sum_x \mathrm{row}( x ) \f$ the extractions cost
 \f$ O( W / B + 2^B W / ( 64 B ) ) \f$ amortized, the second term the scan of
 the mask; the keys of a band of width \f$ C \f$ above \f$ \ell \f$ use only
 the lowest \f$ \lceil \log_2 ( C + 1 ) / B \rceil \f$ rows, and with
 \f$ B = 1 \f$ an extraction costs \f$ O( \log C ) \f$ amortized.

 The two levels of `BucketQueue.h` apply the scan of Dial to two keys, the
 first \f$ f \f$ of the order and the second \f$ g \f$ that breaks its ties
 towards the largest, which is the order of the search A* (least \f$ f \f$,
 ties towards the deeper nodes). The outer level is a vector of
 <em>layers</em> indexed by \f$ f \f$, scanned forward from the least
 \f$ f \f$ in use; the scan only moves forward between two pushes of a smaller
 \f$ f \f$, and with keys bounded by \f$ C \f$ it costs \f$ O( 1 ) \f$
 amortized per operation. A layer, the items of one \f$ f \f$, keeps a LIFO
 list per \f$ g \f$, in one of two ways. The lists of \f$ g < D \f$
 (\f$ D = 256 \f$) are <em>dense</em>, an array of heads indexed by
 \f$ g \f$ and scanned backward from a bound that only moves down between two
 pushes of a larger \f$ g \f$. The lists of \f$ g \ge D \f$ are
 <em>runs</em> while they are at most \f$ R = 64 \f$: a vector of pairs
 \f$ ( g , h ) \f$, \f$ h \f$ the head of the list of \f$ g \f$, sorted by
 increasing \f$ g \f$, whose last pair is the top of the layer. A push of a
 \f$ g \f$ larger than the last appends a run, a push of the last
 \f$ g \f$ prepends to its list, and any other push finds its place by a
 binary search and shifts the larger runs by one place. A push of a new
 \f$ g \ge D \f$ into a layer of \f$ R \f$ runs turns the layer dense on all
 its \f$ g \f$: the heads of the runs, not the items, move into an array as
 long as the largest \f$ g \f$, and the layer stays dense until it empties,
 when it frees the array and is sparse again.

 <em>Lemma 5 (the cost of a layer).</em> Between two times at which a layer is
 empty, \f$ m \f$ pushes into it and as many pops cost \f$ O( m + D ) \f$ if
 its items have at most \f$ R \f$ distinct \f$ g \ge D \f$, and
 \f$ O( m + D + G ) \f$ otherwise, \f$ G \f$ the largest \f$ g \f$ pushed
 into it, i.e., \f$ O( 1 + ( D + G ) / R ) \f$ per push in the second case.

 Indeed, a push to the runs costs \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ when its \f$ g \f$ is not
 below the last, and \f$ O( \log R + R ) = O( 1 ) \f$ otherwise, since
 \f$ R \f$ is a constant and the shift moves at most \f$ R \f$ runs, while a
 pop takes the head of the last run in \f$ O( 1 ) \f$; the dense lists of the
 \f$ g < D \f$ cost at most \f$ D \f$ for their scan between two fillings.
 Only the conversion and the array that follows it cost \f$ \Theta( G ) \f$,
 to allocate, to set and to scan down, and the conversion comes only with the
 \f$ ( R + 1 ) \f$-th distinct \f$ g \ge D \f$, i.e., after more than
 \f$ R \f$ pushes. A dense array alone would instead cost
 \f$ \Theta( G ) \f$ at each filling, even of one item, which is the case of
 the least layer of a search whose nodes come in small batches; a binary heap
 per layer would cost \f$ O( \log m ) \f$ per operation, more than the dense
 lists on a layer of many items, and would lose the order of the lists, which
 the view of the standard interface walks.

 The order is the same in both ways (LIFO inside a list, the lists by
 \f$ g \f$), since the conversion moves the heads and not the items. The
 items lie in one vector of cells, each with the index of the next cell of its
 list, and the cells freed by the pops form a list that the pushes take from
 first, so that no operation calls the allocator once the vector has grown to
 the largest size of the queue; the vectors of runs of the layers that empty
 are kept for the next layers that need one. The memory is
 \f[
   O\Bigl( n + F + \sum_f H_f + R L \Bigr) \text{ words,}
 \f]
 \f$ F \f$ the largest \f$ f \f$ pushed, \f$ H_f \f$ the heads of the layer
 \f$ f \f$ while it is in use (at most \f$ D \f$ if it is sparse, its
 largest \f$ g \f$ if it is dense) and \f$ L \f$ the most layers that had
 runs at the same time. There is no `decrease()`: as in the lazy deletion of
 A*, an item whose key improves is pushed again, and the caller skips the
 stale copies when they come out.

 \section theory_priority_double Double-ended queues

 A double-ended queue has both the best and the worst item at hand
 (`MinMaxHeap.h`). The min-max heap of Atkinson, Sack, Santoro and
 Strothotte is a binary heap in a vector whose levels alternate: a node of
 an even level, the root's, is not worse than any item of its subtree, and
 one of an odd level is not better. The best item is then the root and the
 worst the worse of its children. An item pushed is swapped at most once
 with its parent, to reach a level of the right kind, and then moved up by
 its grandparents; an item moved down from the root (or from the child of
 the worst) is compared with the up to 2 children and 4 grandchildren, and
 swapped with its parent of the other kind when it lands on a grandchild.
 Both walks cost \f$ O( \log n ) \f$.

 The interval heap of van Leeuwen and Wood keeps the items two by two in the
 nodes of a complete binary tree, each node an interval
 \f$ [ \mathit{lo} , \mathit{hi} ] \f$ with
 \f[
   \mathit{lo}( q ) \preceq \mathit{lo}( j ) \preceq \mathit{hi}( j )
   \preceq \mathit{hi}( q ) , \qquad q \text{ the parent of } j , \tag{6}
 \f]
 \f$ \preceq \f$ the order of `better()`. Hence the \f$ \mathit{lo} \f$ of
 the nodes form a heap for the best item and the \f$ \mathit{hi} \f$ a heap
 for the worst, and a walk of either kind costs two comparisons per level
 on a tree of half the height of a binary heap, with the two items of a
 node in one line of the cache. In both heaps, a run of removals with no
 push, as long as an eighth of the items, sorts the items once in
 \f$ O( n \log n ) \f$ (or by the radix sort, \f$ O( n ) \f$, on numbers),
 so that the rest of the run takes them from the two ends of the sorted
 array in \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ each; the next push rebuilds a heap in
 \f$ O( n ) \f$. The run pays for both, since it holds
 \f$ \Omega( n ) \f$ removals, and the amortized cost of a removal stays
 \f$ O( \log n ) \f$.

 \section theory_priority_merge Merging sequences and the cache

 The merge of \f$ k \f$ sorted sequences extracts at each step the least of
 their first items; a heap of the \f$ k \f$ first items does it with a
 `deleteMin()` and an `insert()`, up to \f$ 2 \log_2 k \f$ comparisons. The
 tree of losers of a tournament (`LoserTree.h`, Knuth 5.4.1) does better:
 the \f$ k \f$ sequences are the leaves of a complete binary tree, each
 internal node keeps the loser of the match played there, and the root the
 overall winner. When the winner takes its next item, only the matches on
 the path from its leaf to the root change, and each is played against the
 loser stored there, one comparison per level; hence a merge of \f$ n \f$
 items costs at most
 \f[
   n \lceil \log_2 k \rceil + k \tag{7}
 \f]
 comparisons, the term \f$ k \f$ for the construction, which plays the
 \f$ k - 1 \f$ matches once. Equal keys are won by the lower index, so that
 the merge is stable. `FlatLoserTree` of `MultiwayMerge.h` keeps the key of
 each loser in its node, so that a replay reads only the array of the tree,
 and makes the matches and the swaps by masks, since a branch would be
 mispredicted half of the time on random keys. The funnel of
 `multiwayMerge()` (the cache-oblivious \f$ k \f$-merger of Frigo,
 Leiserson, Prokop and Ramachandran, made of binary mergers as Brodal,
 Fagerberg and Vinther engineered it) is a tree of binary mergers with a
 buffer at each node, filled lazily when it runs out; each item climbs
 \f$ \lceil \log_2 k \rceil \f$ mergers, \f$ O( n \log k ) \f$ in all, and
 the steps of a merge of two check no end and make no jump.

 In the model of the cache, with \f$ M \f$ items in the cache and \f$ B \f$
 in a block, a heap of \f$ n \gg M \f$ items misses the cache at almost
 every level of a walk, i.e., \f$ \Theta( \log ( n / M ) ) \f$ times per
 operation. The sequence heap of Sanders (`SequenceHeap.h`) keeps instead
 the items in sorted sequences, merged in large batches: the new items go
 to an insertion heap of \f$ m = \Theta( M ) \f$ items, which, when full, is
 sorted into a sequence of the group 1; the group \f$ i \f$ holds up to
 \f$ k = \Theta( M / B ) \f$ sequences of at most \f$ m k^{i-1} \f$ items,
 and when it is full its sequences are merged into one of the group
 \f$ i + 1 \f$; the best items wait in a deletion buffer, refilled by
 merging the buffers of the groups. Each item is merged once per group, by
 a tree of losers of \f$ k \f$ sequences, and the sequences are read and
 written in order; hence the operations cost \f$ O( \log n ) \f$ amortized
 comparisons, as a binary heap, and
 \f[
   O\left( \frac{1}{B} \log_{M / B} \frac{n}{M} \right) \tag{8}
 \f]
 amortized misses per item (Sanders, theorem 1), i.e., the bound of the
 sorting in external memory divided by the items. The pushes into an empty
 sequence heap go to a bulk in the order of arrival, which the incremental
 quicksort of Paredes and Navarro sorts only as far as the extractions
 need, \f$ O( n + k \log k ) \f$ expected for the \f$ k \f$ best items of
 \f$ n \f$.

